Specification AcceptanceAgainstQuicken

Description Comparing the figures SimpleAccount reports with the figures Quicken
Description reports for the same data over the same period.
Details
Details Quicken is the oracle. For the migration there is no other one: the only
Details way to know that twenty years came over correctly is that the totals
Description agree with the program the data came from.
Details
Details What is compared is amounts. Font, column widths, page breaks, row order
Details and the wording of headings are not compared and not expected to match.
Details A line of a Quicken report and a line of a SimpleAccount report are the
Details same line when they are about the same category over the same period.
Details
Details The expected figures come from PDF reports exported from Quicken. A PDF
Details is not machine-readable in any way worth relying on, so the figures are
Details transcribed into the tables below and the tables become the test. That
Details is the point of writing a specification as tables: the report from
Details Quicken is already a table of examples.
Details
Details This round compares spending reports: what was spent and earned, by
Details category and by payee, over a period. Net worth, account balances and
Details anything about investment performance are not compared yet.
Details
Details That does not make the investment records ignorable. A dividend posts to
Details an income category and a brokerage fee to an expense one, so 899
Details investment records in the business file reach the spending report
Details through their income and expense sides. What is out of scope is
Details reporting on the holdings, not importing what they paid.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is being compared
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
DataType ComparisonRun
Description The comparisons to be made, in the order they are worth making.
Details Each later one depends on the one before it passing: there is no point
Details comparing a single year until the whole period agrees, because a
Details difference in the whole period tells you less about where it is.
Details These are the four PDFs in testdata, in the order they are worth
Details running. The last two are a pair: the same report before and after the
Details three QFX files are imported, so the difference between them is
Details exactly what the import did.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value         | Notes                                                           |
| WholePeriod   | All Dates, 1/1/2005 to 10/3/2026; catches a systematic error     |
| FullYear2025  | Last year, 1/1/2025 to 12/31/2025; no partial-period effects     |
| CurrentYear   | Year to Date, 1/1/2026 to 10/3/2026, before the QFX import       |
| AfterQfx      | Year to Date again, after importing the three QFX files          |
| PerYear       | One calendar year; the From and To of the report say which       |

Entity QuickenReport
Description One PDF report from Quicken, and what it is a report of.
| Attribute  | Type           | Default      | Notes                           |
| Run        | ComparisonRun  | WholePeriod  |                                 |
| Book       | Text           |              | Which QIF file it belongs to    |
| ReportKind | QuickenReportKind | SpendingByCategory |                           |
| From       | Date           | 2005-01-01   |                                 |
| To         | Date           | 2026-10-03   |                                 |
| Lines      | Integer        | 0            | How many figure lines it has     |

$WIP
DataType QuickenReportKind
Description Which Quicken report the PDF is of. It matters: the reports differ
Description in what they include, not only in how they look.
Details This round compares spending reports and nothing else. The balance and
Details net worth reports are listed so that the inclusion rules can say what
Details they would do, and they are not among the runs below.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value              | Notes                                                 |
| SpendingByCategory | In scope; the report this round is about              |
| SpendingByPayee    | In scope; the same figures grouped the other way      |
| ItemizedCategories | In scope; every transaction, grouped by category      |
| CashFlow           | In scope; money in and out by category                |
| NetWorth           | Not compared this round                               |
| AccountBalances    | Not compared this round                               |

Attributes ComparisonLine
Description One line of the comparison: what Quicken said, what SimpleAccount
Description said, and whether they agree.
| Attribute      | Type        | Default |
| QuickenLine    | Text        |         |
| OurAccount     | AccountPath | none    |
| QuickenAmount  | Money       | 0.00    |
| OurAmount      | Money       | 0.00    |
| Difference     | Money       | 0.00    |
| Agrees         | Boolean     | false   |

Attributes ComparisonSummary
| Attribute       | Type    | Default |
| LinesCompared   | Integer | 0       |
| LinesAgreeing   | Integer | 0       |
| LinesDiffering  | Integer | 0       |
| OnlyInQuicken   | Integer | 0       |
| OnlyInOurReport | Integer | 0       |
| TotalDifference | Money   | 0.00    |
| Passes          | Boolean | false   |

$WIP
BusinessRule When a comparison passes
Description Every line agrees to the cent, and neither report has a line the
Description other does not.
Details There is no tolerance. A difference of a cent is a rounding rule
Details somewhere that disagrees, and over twenty years a rounding rule that
Details disagrees will not stay at a cent. A line present in one report and
Details not the other is a failure even when the amount is zero, because it
Details means a category was created or dropped.
Examples: PassCondition
| LinesDiffering | OnlyInQuicken | OnlyInOurReport | Passes | Notes                    |
| 0              | 0             | 0               | true   |                          |
| 1              | 0             | 0               | false  | One figure disagrees     |
| 0              | 1             | 0               | false  | A category went missing  |
| 0              | 0             | 1               | false  | A category was invented  |

Attributes PassCondition
| Attribute       | Type    | Default |
| LinesDiffering  | Integer | 0       |
| OnlyInQuicken   | Integer | 0       |
| OnlyInOurReport | Integer | 0       |
| Passes          | Boolean | false   |
| Notes           | String  | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lining the two reports up
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
BusinessRule How a Quicken category name becomes an account path
Description Quicken reports a category by the name the category list gives it,
Description with no root. SimpleAccount puts Income or Expenses in front. The
Description comparison adds the root back, using the income or expense flag
Description from the same category list the migration used.
Examples: ReportLineMapping
| QuickenLine      | OurAccount             | Notes                           |
| Groceries        | Expenses:Groceries     |                                 |
| Auto:Fuel        | Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Subcategories keep their depth  |
| Auto             | Expenses:Auto          | A parent with its own total     |
| Salary           | Income:Salary          |                                 |
| TOTAL Auto       | Expenses:Auto          | A subtotal line, same account   |
| TOTAL INFLOWS    | Income                 | The root subtotal               |
| TOTAL OUTFLOWS   | Expenses               |                                 |
| OVERALL TOTAL    | Net                    | Compared with Net, not an account |

Attributes ReportLineMapping
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| QuickenLine | Text        |         |
| OurAccount  | AccountPath | none    |
| Notes       | String      | none    |

$WIP
BusinessRule How the signs of the two reports are lined up
Description Quicken writes an Itemized Categories report in natural signs:
Description income positive, expenses negative, and the overall total their sum.
Description SimpleAccount reports both as positive and nets them, because that
Description is easier to read down a column. So expense lines are negated on
Description one side of the comparison.
Details Observed in all three populated PDFs. The all-dates report has INCOME
Details 3,742,439.40, EXPENSES -2,715,446.00, TRANSFERS -63,888.84, and an
Details OVERALL TOTAL of 963,104.56, which is their sum exactly.
Details Getting this wrong would flip several hundred lines at once, which is
Details why it is a rule with examples rather than a remark.
Examples: SignMapping
| Section   | QuickenSign | OurSign  | Comparison                        |
| INCOME    | Positive    | Positive | Compared as they stand            |
| EXPENSES  | Negative    | Positive | Our figure is negated to compare  |
| TRANSFERS | Signed      | Signed   | Compared as they stand            |
| OVERALL   | Sum of the three | Income less expenses | Computed each way and compared |

Attributes SignMapping
| Attribute   | Type   | Default |
| Section     | String |         |
| QuickenSign | String |         |
| OurSign     | String |         |
| Comparison  | String |         |

$WIP
Scenario The section totals of the all-dates report
Description The three section totals and the overall total, transcribed from
Description pughkillleen-allyears-category.pdf. This is the single comparison
Description that says the migration worked, and it is four figures.
